<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constitution of Japan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as constitution of 1947</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> was adopted in 1947 under the Allied occupation after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, replacing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-constitution">Meiji Constitution</a> of 1889. It made the emperor a symbol of the state with no political power and established popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. It also renounced war as a sovereign right, marking the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constitution-of-japan.jpg" alt="Constitution of Japan" width="292" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Japanese government · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nihon_Kenpo01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-constitution">Meiji Constitution</a> of 1889, the fundamental law of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>, was modelled on Prussian and British examples and made the prime minister and cabinet accountable to the emperor rather than to the elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>. On 26 July 1945 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the United Kingdom and the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam-declaration">Potsdam Declaration</a>, which demanded surrender, demilitarisation and democratisation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> accepted its conditions, which implied changes to the constitution after the surrender.</p><p>General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douglas-macarthur">Douglas MacArthur</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/supreme-commander-for-the-allied-powers">Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers</a>, at first directed Prime Minister Kijūrō Shidehara to prepare a new constitution, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hirohito">Emperor Hirohito</a> and most of the cabinet were reluctant to replace the 1889 document. In February 1946 MacArthur presented a draft prepared under his own supervision, which Japanese scholars reviewed and modified. To preserve legal continuity, the new text was adopted as an amendment to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-constitution">Meiji Constitution</a> under Article 73 of that document.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The constitution has a preamble and 103 articles in 11 chapters, and is short at about 5,000 words. The preamble declares that sovereign power resides with the people. The emperor is 'the symbol of the State and of the unity of the people', and his role is entirely ceremonial. Article 9 states that the Japanese people forever renounce war as a sovereign right of the nation and the threat or use of force to settle international disputes.</p><p>Thirty-one articles describe the rights and duties of the people, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-constitution">Meiji Constitution</a> had granted only within the limits of law. The constitution sets up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary system</a>. A bicameral National Diet is made up of a House of Representatives and a House of Councillors, and the old House of Peers, composed of nobles, was abolished. The Cabinet is headed by a Prime Minister and answerable to the Diet. Amendments need a two-thirds vote of each house and a majority in a referendum.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Diet's two houses made amendments of their own without interference from MacArthur, adding provisions for suits against the State for officials' torts and for wrongful detention. The text took effect on 3 May 1947, after a six-month transitional period. A government body, the Kenpō Fukyū Kai, was established to promote acceptance of the new constitution among the people.</p><p>The necessity and extent of Article 9 have been debated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> since its enactment, particularly after the Self-Defense Forces were set up in 1954. Some lower courts have found them unconstitutional, but the Supreme Court has never ruled on the question.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Constitution of Japan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Formal capitulation was signed aboard USS Missouri on 2 September 1945, and the <b>constitution of 1947</b> renounced war and reduced the emperor to a symbol of the state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>constitution of 1947</b> created a parliamentary union with semi-autonomous ethnic states, and U Nu governed through the AFPFL until it split in 1958, when General Ne Win led a caretaker government for eighteen months.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Japan’s <b>constitution of 1947</b> made the emperor “the symbol of the State and of the unity of the people”.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1945 parliamentary democracy was rebuilt under Allied occupation in Italy, in West Germany, whose Basic Law of 1949 introduced the constructive vote of no confidence, and in Japan, whose <b>constitution of 1947</b> made the cabinet answerable to the Diet.</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>A <b>constitution of 1947</b> made Laos a constitutional monarchy within the French Union, and full independence came in 1953.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-laos">Kingdom of Laos</a></li></ul></section>
